#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:270
msgid "6 View the message definition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:272
msgid ""
"Nodes send data over topics by using messages. To communicate, publishers "
"and subscribers must send and receive the same type of message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:275
msgid ""
"The topic types you see after running ``ros2 topic list -t`` tell you what "
"message type is used on each topic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:277
msgid ""
"For example, the ``cmd_vel`` topic has the type ``geometry_msgs/msg/Twist``. "
"Here, ``geometry_msgs`` is the package and ``Twist`` is the message "
"definition within it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:280
msgid ""
"Now you can run ``ros2 interface show <msg_type>`` on this type to learn its "
"details. Specifically, what structure of data the message expects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:283
msgid "To learn the details of the ``Twist`` message, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:285
msgid "$ ros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/Twist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:289
msgid "The command will return:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:291
msgid ""
"# This expresses velocity in free space broken into its linear and angular "
"parts.\n"
"    Vector3  linear\n"
"            float64 x\n"
"            float64 y\n"
"            float64 z\n"
"    Vector3  angular\n"
"            float64 x\n"
"            float64 y\n"
"            float64 z"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:303
msgid ""
"This tells you that the ``/turtlesim`` node is expecting a message with two "
"vectors, ``linear`` and ``angular``, of three elements each. The data that "
"``ros2 topic echo`` printed earlier follows the same structure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:319
msgid "7 Publish data to a topic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:321
msgid ""
"Now that you have the message structure, you can publish data to a topic "
"directly from the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:323
msgid "To do this, use the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:325
msgid "$ ros2 topic pub <topic_name> <msg_type> '<args>'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:329
msgid ""
"The ``'<args>'`` argument is the actual data passed to the topic, in the "
"structure shown by ``ros2 interface show`` in `6 View the message "
"definition`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:331
msgid "There are four main ways to use the ``pub`` command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:333
msgid "**Publish dictionary strings**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:335
msgid ""
"In order to publish data to a topic, you need to pass the data in the form "
"of YAML strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:337
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:464
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \"{linear: {x: "
"2.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}, angular: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 1.8}}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:341
msgid ""
"However, you do not need to specify the entire message. If you are only "
"changing the linear or angular velocity, specify just the values you want to "
"change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:344
msgid ""
"For example, if you want to change the linear velocity to 2.0 and keep the "
"angular velocity at 1.8, you can do the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:346
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \"{linear: {x: "
"2.0}, angular: {z: 1.8}}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:350
msgid "**Publishing an empty message**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:352
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:372
msgid "$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:356
msgid ""
"This will publish the default values for the message type at the default "
"rate of 1 Hz, which is equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:358
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \"{linear: {x: "
"0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}, angular: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}}\" --rate 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:362
msgid "**Use auto-complete**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:365
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:438
msgid ""
"This feature is not supported on Windows. For other operating systems, you "
"must configure ``argcomplete``. :doc:`Sourcing the ROS setup script "
"<../../../../Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment>` sets this up "
"automatically. If ``argcomplete`` is active in your shell, then :kbd:`Tab` "
"completion provides command options, not file paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:370
msgid ""
"You can trigger the auto-complete feature of your terminal by entering the "
"following, then pressing :kbd:`Tab` instead of :kbd:`Enter`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:376
msgid "The terminal shows the available options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:378
msgid ""
"--keep-alive\n"
"--max-wait-time-secs\n"
"--node-name\n"
"--once\n"
"--print\n"
"--qos-depth\n"
"--qos-durability\n"
"--qos-history\n"
"--qos-liveliness\n"
"--qos-liveliness-lease-duration-seconds\n"
"--qos-profile\n"
"--qos-reliability\n"
"--rate\n"
"--spin-time\n"
"--stdin\n"
"--times\n"
"--use-sim-time\n"
"--wait-matching-subscriptions\n"
"--yaml-file\n"
"-1\n"
"-n\n"
"-p\n"
"-r\n"
"-s\n"
"-t\n"
"-w\n"
"\\'linear:\\^J\\ \\ x:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^Jangular:"
"\\^J\\ \\ x:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^J\\'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:408
msgid ""
"When you press :kbd:`Tab` after entering the first few characters of an "
"option, the terminal auto-completes it. The last item in the options list is "
"a YAML template of the message fields with default values. To insert that "
"template, enter an escaped single quote, then press :kbd:`Tab`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:412
msgid "$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \\'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:416
msgid ""
"The terminal does not recognise an unescaped single quote as part of the "
"auto-complete string, so you need to escape it with ``\\'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:418
msgid "The final auto-completed string should look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:420
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist 'linear:\n"
"  x: 0.0\n"
"  y: 0.0\n"
"  z: 0.0\n"
"angular:\n"
"  x: 0.0\n"
"  y: 0.0\n"
"  z: 0.0\n"
"'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:432
msgid ""
"This string is editable. Change the field values as required before you run "
"the command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:435
msgid "**Use the raw auto-completed string**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:443
msgid ""
"The auto-completed string for ``geometry_msgs/msg/Twist`` looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:445
msgid ""
"\\'linear:\\^J\\ \\ x:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^Jangular:"
"\\^J\\ \\ x:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^J\\'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:449
msgid ""
"You can use this string directly in place of the YAML string on the command "
"line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:451
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \\'linear:\\^J\\ "
"\\ x:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^Jangular:\\^J\\ \\ x:\\ "
"0.0\\^J\\ \\ y:\\ 0.0\\^J\\ \\ z:\\ 0.0\\^J\\'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:455
msgid ""
"This publishes the default field values for the message type. For ``Twist``, "
"those defaults are all zero values, so if you run this command exactly as "
"shown, the turtle doesn't move."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:458
msgid ""
"To make the turtle move, before you run the command, replace the zero values "
"with the velocities you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:461
msgid ""
"The turtle, like physical robots, requires a steady stream of commands to "
"operate continuously. To keep the turtle moving continuously, use non-zero "
"values for the velocities, like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:470
msgid ""
"Sometimes you may want to publish data to your topic only once rather than "
"continuously. To publish your command only once, add the ``--once`` option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:473
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub --once -w 2 /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist "
"\"{linear: {x: 2.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}, angular: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 1.8}}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:477
msgid ""
"``--once`` is an optional argument that means \"publish one message then "
"exit\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:479
msgid ""
"``-w 2`` is an optional argument that means \"wait for two matching "
"subscriptions\". The argument is needed because you have both ``turtlesim`` "
"and the topic ``echo`` subscribed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:482
msgid "You should see the following output in the terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:484
msgid ""
"Waiting for at least 2 matching subscription(s)...\n"
"publisher: beginning loop\n"
"publishing #1: geometry_msgs.msg.Twist(linear=geometry_msgs.msg."
"Vector3(x=2.0, y=0.0, z=0.0), angular=geometry_msgs.msg.Vector3(x=0.0, "
"y=0.0, z=1.8))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:490
msgid "You should see your turtle move like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:494
msgid ""
"You can refresh ``rqt_graph`` to see what's happening graphically. You "
"should see that the ``ros2 topic pub ...`` node (``/_ros2cli_30358``) is "
"publishing over the ``/turtle1/cmd_vel`` topic, which is now being received "
"by both the ``ros2 topic echo ...`` node (``/_ros2cli_26646``) and the ``/"
"turtlesim`` node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:499
msgid ""
"Finally, to see the ``/turtlesim`` node publishing on the ``pose`` topic, "
"run ``echo`` on it and refresh ``rqt_graph``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:501
msgid "$ ros2 topic echo /turtle1/pose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:507
msgid ""
"You can see that the ``/turtlesim`` node is also publishing to the ``pose`` "
"topic, which the new ``echo`` node has subscribed to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:509
msgid ""
"When publishing messages with timestamps, ``pub`` has two methods to "
"automatically fill them out with the current time:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:511
msgid ""
"For messages with a ``std_msgs/msg/Header``, set the header field to "
"``auto`` to fill out the ``stamp`` field:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:513
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /pose geometry_msgs/msg/PoseStamped '{header: \"auto\", "
"pose: {position: {x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0}}}'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:517
msgid ""
"If the message has a ``builtin_interfaces/msg/Time`` field instead of a full "
"header, set that field to ``now``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:519
msgid ""
"$ ros2 topic pub /reference sensor_msgs/msg/TimeReference '{header: "
"\"auto\", time_ref: \"now\", source: \"dumy\"}'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:524
msgid "8 View the rate at which data is published"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:526
msgid "To view the rate at which data is published, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:528
msgid "$ ros2 topic hz /turtle1/pose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:532
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:555
msgid "You should see output similar to the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:534
msgid ""
"average rate: 59.354\n"
"  min: 0.005s max: 0.027s std dev: 0.00284s window: 58"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:539
msgid ""
"The command will return data on the rate at which the ``/turtlesim`` node is "
"publishing data to the ``pose`` topic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:541
msgid ""
"To check the rate of ``turtle1/cmd_vel``, which was set to 1 Hz using ``ros2 "
"topic pub --rate 1``, run ``ros2 topic hz /turtle1/cmd_vel`` and the average "
"should reflect that rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:544
msgid ""
"The rate reflects the receiving rate on the subscription created by the "
"``ros2 topic hz`` command, which might be affected by platform resources and "
"QoS configuration, and may not exactly match the publisher rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:547
msgid "9 View the bandwidth utilization of a topic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:549
msgid "To view the bandwidth used by a topic, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.rst:551
